From d345a538529be106cf81633eb5e1b325546f2162 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:35:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: Dolby Vision that VAAPI does not decode says
 why; DRM PRIME leaves what it cannot decode

What Kodi's decoder settings do to Dolby Vision here, read in the code and measured where it can be:
 - A Dolby Vision stream that is decoded for native output or for output as HDR10 goes to
   CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg whatever "Allow using DRM PRIME decoder" says (CDVDFactoryCodec), and its
   pictures to CRendererVAAPIGLES (the renderers are chosen by the kind of picture). So DRM PRIME on
   changes nothing for it: the bridge still imports its VAAPI surfaces.
 - "Allow hardware acceleration - VAAPI" off (or off for its codec, or a VAAPI that cannot decode the
   stream) has its pictures decoded by the processor: Kodi's ordinary renderer draws them, natively it
   cannot (CanRenderDVBridge()), and the enhancement layer is not decoded. The video plays as Kodi plays
   it, the Dolby Vision page said only "not available for this video", and a profile 5 stream, which only
   the reconstruction shows right, shows wrong colours.
 - Every other stream with DRM PRIME on goes to CDVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME. This build's FFmpeg has no decoder
   that decodes to DRM PRIME in hardware (no V4L2 M2M, no V4L2 request), so it decodes in software, into
   the 8-bit formats of IsSupportedSwFormat() only. A 10-bit stream (HDR10, HLG, Dolby Vision that is not
   native) finds no format: its software path, run on the host with the HEVC Main 10 base layer of a Dolby
   Vision stream, gets EPERM from avcodec_send_packet() for every packet and no picture, and AddData()
   then returns false, which makes VideoPlayer send the same packet again and again (8-bit H.264 decodes).

Now:
 - The first picture of such a Dolby Vision stream tells whether it came from VAAPI (DVBRIDGE::
   DecoderTook()): when not, the log says why ("Allow hardware acceleration - VAAPI" off, VAAPI off for its
   codec, VAAPI could not decode it, no VAAPI) and that native output and the reconstruction need VAAPI,
   and the Dolby Vision page of the player information gives that reason (vaapioff, vaapicodec, software;
   Estuary 31917-31919) until the next video. The user's setting is kept: VAAPI is not turned on behind it.
 - CDVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME declines a stream that no decoder decodes to DRM PRIME in hardware when it has
   more than 8 bits (the depth the demuxer found, or a profile of more bits where that is not known;
   DVBRIDGE::PrimeSoftwareTakes()): the factory gives it to the next decoder, which decodes it as with
   DRM PRIME off. 8-bit video keeps the software DRM PRIME path as before.
 - The help of the two settings says what they mean for Dolby Vision here, after Kodi's own text (core
   39951 and 39952, Estuary 31915 and 31916); the note of DRM PRIME only where no decoder of the build
   decodes to it in hardware (DVBRIDGE::PrimeHardwareDecoders()).

The defaults of Generic, from LibreELEC's appliance-gbm-generic.xml (DRM PRIME off, its hardware
acceleration off, the GLES render method; VAAPI on, as Kodi has it), are the right ones for native Dolby
Vision and stay as they are.
